coercive control has been an offence in  the uk since  2015 but what is it is the law working  and how can you spot whether it's  happening to you  under the serious crimes act the  following must be true for someone to be  convicted of this offence  that the abuser is personally connected  to the victim their behavior has had a  serious effect on the victim and the  abuser knew or ought to have known that  their behavior would have a serious  effect on you your abuser's behavior is  considered to have a serious effect if  on at least two occasions the victim has  feared that violence will be used  against them or they have felt serious  alarm or distress and it has had a  substantial effect on their usual  day-to-day activities before this law  the only way that domestic abusers were  convicted was if they were physically  assaulting victims but coercive control  is far more subtle than that and more  difficult to spot especially as victims  become further detached from family and  friends  the impact of coercive control varies  between victims but a really common  response is post traumatic stress  disorder this can physically alter the  victim's brain and ability to function  the amygdala which triggers the body's  responses to stress is put on high alert  by the constant abuse and can actively  warp a person's ability to react  rationally and see things clearly  [Music]  because of the nature of this crime  victims themselves might not even know  what's happening to them  here are the key signs that's happening  to you or someone you know  isolating you from friends and family  monitoring your time spying on your  social media or devices taking control  over aspects of your everyday life such  as where you can go who you can see what  you can wear and when you can sleep  depriving you access to support services  such as medical  repeatedly putting you down such as  saying you're worthless humiliating  degrading or dehumanizing you  controlling your finances  making threats or intimidating you  depriving you of basic needs  [Music]  mental abuse is the most difficult thing  to  explain to people i think that over the  years mine  was like a drip feed  coming at me  and it came to a crescendo at the end  sally chalan had been convicted of  murdering her husband who'd emotionally  abused her for many years in a landmark  decision her murder conviction was  overturned in 2019 thanks to this law  the court took into consideration that  she had been coercively controlled the  court that convicted her wasn't able to  take into account the psychological  effect that being in that sort of  abusive relationship will have had on  her and on her mental state  and the state she was in when she killed  her partner  while her case showed just how powerful  an impact coercive control could have on  victims the law hasn't transformed the  justice system in the way that some  campaigners would have wanted the number  of coercive control offences recorded by  the police increased from 4246  in 12 months across 2016 and 2017  to 24  856  in 12 months across 2019 and 2020  in 2019 just 1  112 defendants were prosecuted for these  offenses  a government review acknowledged that  the crime is likely to be far more  prevalent but there are issues in both  victims and police recognizing the crime  i think what is necessary rather than  more legislation  is better training  for  police of recognizing when the situation  is dangerous and when an offense needs  to be charged  if you want to speak to someone about  the issues we've raised here are some  numbers you can call
 

